EC2 readiness audit

Hardcoded local addresses
harness/src/sender.cpp line 32 defaults --host to 127.0.0.1.
benchmark/run_once.sh line 12 defaults host=127.0.0.1.
Receiver default bind is 0.0.0.0 in receiver.cpp line 27, which is acceptable for remote senders.
The sender accepts remote destinations via --host and via repeated --dst. sender.cpp lines 97-100 parse host:port, line 118 accepts --dst, and line 141 falls back to --host/--port only when no --dst is supplied.
benchmark/run_once.sh lines 127-131 collect repeated --dst values and lines 232-238 pass them to the sender.

Fan-out testing
No unit test or harness test currently exercises multiple --dst fan-out. Existing tests cover library logic and local single-destination runs only. Multi-destination EC2 behavior is NOT_RUN.

Clock source risk
util::now_ns uses std::chrono::system_clock in util.h lines 8-12.
Producer timestamps are written with util::now_ns in producer.cpp and consumer latency subtracts those timestamps on the receiving host. Across two EC2 hosts, wall-clock skew makes end-to-end latency invalid unless clocks are synchronized tightly or the measurement is redesigned. This is the main two-host correctness risk for latency numbers. It does not break seq_id/FEC/dedupe correctness.

Network impairment dependency
The seeded sender injector is in-process and does not require sch_netem. sch_netem is not required for the offline seeded impairment tests. tc/netem remains optional if the final EC2 methodology wants kernel-level impairment instead of sender-side deterministic drop/reorder.

Fresh Ubuntu 24.04 dependencies likely needed
build-essential or g++
make
python3
git
openssh-client for GitLab SSH remote operations
taskset from util-linux for benchmark/run_once.sh CPU pinning
iproute2 only if tc/netem is used
standard libc/POSIX shared memory support, /dev/shm mounted

Script portability risks
benchmark/run_once.sh writes default results under benchmark/results/run_TIMESTAMP and assumes a local role=all workflow unless role rx/tx is explicitly split.
The script removes /dev/shm entries for both input and output SHM names on the host where it runs. In two-host mode, the SHM rings are host-local and must be orchestrated separately.
CPU IDs default to 1,2,4,6 and may not exist on smaller EC2 instances.
Socket buffer defaults are 4194304 bytes but sysctl max values on a fresh image may cap requested buffers lower.
MTU, ENA driver behavior, security group ingress, and UDP receive buffer drops can differ from WSL2 loopback.
